// %% [markdown]
// # Lomb–Scargle EOFs: spectra of a gappy 2D+time field, then complex EOFs by SVD
//
// Skeleton notebook after **Dupuis & Schumacher (2018)**, *Using Lomb–Scargle
// Analysis to Derive Empirical Orthogonal Functions from Gappy Meteorological
// Data* (J. Appl. Meteor. Climatol. 57, 2217–2229, doi:10.1175/JAMC-D-17-0250.1).
//
// The pipeline, per the paper:
//
// 1. **LSDFT map** — run the complex Lomb–Scargle DFT (`examples/lsdft.blade`)
//    over every point of an unevenly sampled 2D+time field.
// 2. **Diurnal maps** — plot the modulus (relative amplitude) and argument
//    (phase) of the transform at the diurnal frequency.
// 3. **Spectral covariance** — form the Hermitian covariance from the spectra
//    by the cross-correlation theorem (paper Eq. 11): each element is the
//    band-integrated product X̃(ω)·Ỹ*(ω), so only *univariate* transforms are
//    ever computed.
// 4. **Complex EOFs** — diagonalize (for a Hermitian PSD matrix this *is* its
//    SVD) and map the leading EOFs' amplitude and phase.
//
// The field is synthetic — a propagating diurnal cycle, a coast-bound
// semidiurnal harmonic, and a slow 3-day mode, on a gappy jittered 3-hourly
// time base — so every stage has a known right answer. Swap the
// synthetic-field cell for a NetCDF/Zarr provider read to run it on real data.
//
// **On speed.** Notebook cells evaluate under the REPL interpreter, and every
// cell re-interprets the whole accumulated session (`src/Interp/Repl.fs`), so
// each cell costs everything above it. Two structural choices (not resolution
// cuts) keep that in the low seconds at full resolution: the phase table is
// hoisted out of the spatial loop, and the eigenproblem is solved on its small
// side. Both are flagged where they appear.
//
// %%
import units.SI
import math as m
import plot

Unit day = 86400 * second
Unit flux
Unit time: day
Unit angular_frequency: 1 / day

let i = complex(0.0, 1.0)
let two_pi = 6.283185307179586

// Everything is at full resolution: 48 points, 240 samples, 24 frequency bins.
// `n_freq` is the one size that reaches the eigenproblem (see the covariance
// cell), but even so the solve is a 48 x 48 Jacobi — a few million scalar
// operations, i.e. milliseconds of real work.
let static n_lat = 6
let static n_lon = 8
let static n_space = n_lat * n_lon
let static n_t = 240
let static n_freq = 24
let static n_dual = 2 * n_freq
type Lat = Idx<n_lat>
type Lon = Idx<n_lon>
type Space = Idx<n_space>
type Time = Idx<n_t>
type Freq = Idx<n_freq>
type Dual = Idx<n_dual>

let w_d: Float64<angular_frequency> = two_pi   // diurnal: one cycle per day
let f0: Float64<flux> = 1.0
let one_day: Float64<time> = 1.0

// %% [markdown]
// ## A gappy, uneven time base
//
// Sample times are a running sum of jittered 3-hourly increments with
// occasional ~6× bursts — the gaps a satellite orbit leaves. Sequential
// structure is a recursive array in Blade (there is no accumulation loop),
// and recursive arrays carry bare `Float` elements, so the sum runs in days
// and the unit-carrying time base is mapped on afterward.
//
// %%
let rec ts_days: Array<Float64 like Time> = match ts_days with
    | zero -> zero
    | zero :: s -> zero :: 0.0
    | prefix :: n -> prefix :: {
        let jf = 1.0 * n
        let burst = if sin(937.11 * jf + 0.3) > 0.8 then 6.0 else 1.0
        prefix(n - 1) + 0.125 * burst * (1.0 + 0.4 * sin(5.7 * jf + 0.9))
    }

let ts: Array<Float64<time> like Time> = (ts_days <@> lambda(d) -> one_day * d) |> compute

let span_days = reduce(ts_days, lambda(a, b) -> if a > b then a else b)

// %%
span_days

// %% [markdown]
// ## Synthetic 2D+time field
//
// Three modes on a flat `Space` axis (`p = iy * n_lon + ix`), coordinates
// recovered by value arithmetic inside the kernel:
//
// - eastward-propagating **diurnal** cycle with an inland amplitude bump,
// - **semidiurnal** harmonic pinned to the eastern "coast",
// - a slow **3-day** mode, plus deterministic pseudo-noise.
//
// On real data this cell becomes a provider read; everything downstream is
// unchanged.
//
// %%
let lon_count = 1.0 * n_lon
let lat_count = 1.0 * n_lat
let F: Array<Float64<flux> like Space, Time> = method_for(range<Space, Time>) <@> lambda(p, j) -> {
    let pf = 1.0 * p
    let yr = floor(pf / lon_count) / (lat_count - 1.0)
    let xr = (pf - lon_count * floor(pf / lon_count)) / (lon_count - 1.0)
    let td = ts_days(j)
    let a1 = 1.0 + 2.0 * exp(-((xr - 0.35) * (xr - 0.35) + (yr - 0.45) * (yr - 0.45)) / 0.08)
    let p1 = 0.9 * two_pi * xr
    let a2 = 1.2 * exp(-((xr - 0.85) * (xr - 0.85)) / 0.02)
    let p2 = 0.5 * two_pi * yr
    let a3 = 0.6 * (1.0 - yr)
    let p3 = 0.3 * two_pi * yr
    let noise = 0.2 * sin(999.1 * td * (pf + 3.7))
    f0 * (3.0 + a1 * cos(two_pi * td - p1) + a2 * cos(2.0 * two_pi * td - p2) + a3 * cos(two_pi * td / 3.0 - p3) + noise)
} |> compute

// %% [markdown]
// ## The LSDFT and its frequency window
//
// `lsdft` is the phase-preserving generalized Lomb–Scargle DFT from
// `examples/lsdft.blade` (paper Eqs. 14–15, with the τ singularity eliminated
// via ωτ = arg(Z₂)/2).
//
// The window is a uniform comb at 1/12 cycle/day, 24 bins covering periods of
// 12 days down to 12 hours — the paper's 0–12-day band plus the semidiurnal
// harmonic. Each synthetic mode lands exactly on a bin: the 3-day mode at
// index 3, diurnal at 11, semidiurnal at 23.
//
// (Lomb–Scargle imposes no equispacing on the *frequency* axis either, so a
// hand-chosen logarithmic set would cover the same band in half the bins. That
// is worth doing when the band is wide; it is not needed here.)
//
// %%
function variance(x: Array<T like Idx<n>>) = {
    let n = extents(x)
    let d = x - reduce(x, (+)) / n
    prodsum(d, d) / n
}

function lsdft(st_raw: Tuple<U^1, T<time>^1>,
               omegas: T<angular_frequency>^1, t_zero: T<time> = 0.0)
where omp(st_raw: 1) = {
    let s_raw, t_raw = st_raw
    let n = extents(t_raw)
    let s = s_raw - reduce(s_raw, (+)) / n
    let s_var = variance(s)
    let ts = t_raw - t_zero
    omegas <@> lambda(w) where omp(w: 1) -> {
        let e = exp <@> (i * w * ts)
        let Z1 = prodsum(s, e)
        let Z2 = prodsum(e, e)
        let P = Z1 * exp(-i * arg(Z2) / 2)
        let a = real(P) / sqrt((n + abs(Z2)) / 2)
        let b = imag(P) / sqrt((n - abs(Z2)) / 2)
        complex(a, b) * exp(i * w * t_zero) / sqrt(2.0 * s_var)
    }
}

let freqs_cpd: Array<Float64 like Freq> = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> (1.0 + 1.0 * k) / 12.0 |> compute
let omegas: Array<Float64<angular_frequency> like Freq> = (freqs_cpd <@> lambda(f) -> w_d * f) |> compute
let k_diurnal = (11 : Freq)

// %% [markdown]
// ## The LSDFT map over the field
//
// **Hoist what does not vary along the loop axis.** The naive spelling
// recomputes e^{iωt} at every spatial point: O(space · freq · time)
// transcendentals. But `ts` is shared across the field, so the phase table
// `E(k, j)` and `Z₂(k)` depend only on (frequency, sample) and are built
// **once** — O(freq · time). Per-point centering and variance hoist the same
// way. What remains per (point, frequency) is a single `prodsum`, no trig.
// Measured: this cell went from ~15 s to ~0.1 s interpreted, and every cell
// below it stopped re-paying that. Verified digit-for-digit against calling
// `lsdft` per point. (On per-point time bases — truly porous data — the hoist
// is unavailable; that is the price of ragged sampling.)
//
// Two spellings are deliberate, measured against the current compiler: the map
// returns scalars via `range<Space, Freq>` (a kernel returning `lsdft`'s whole
// row leaves the column extent unresolved at codegen), and no generic helper
// is shared with `lsdft` (a helper called from both a generic and a concrete
// function currently loses one of its two monomorphizations).
//
// %%
let E: Array<Complex128 like Freq, Time> = method_for(range<Freq, Time>) <@> lambda(k, j) -> exp(i * omegas(k) * ts(j)) |> compute
let Z2s: Array<Complex128 like Freq> = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> prodsum(E(k), E(k)) |> compute

let s_mean: Array<Float64<flux> like Space> = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> reduce(F(p), (+)) / (1.0 * n_t) |> compute
let Fc: Array<Float64<flux> like Space, Time> = method_for(range<Space, Time>) <@> lambda(p, j) -> F(p, j) - s_mean(p) |> compute
let s_var: Array<Float64 like Space> = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> prodsum(Fc(p), Fc(p)) / (f0 * f0 * n_t) |> compute

function ls_project(sc: Array<Float64<flux> like Idx<n>>, e: Array<Complex128 like Idx<n>>,
                    Z2: Complex128, v: Float64) = {
    let n = extents(sc)
    let Z1 = prodsum(sc, e)
    let P = Z1 * exp(-i * arg(Z2) / 2)
    let a = real(P) / sqrt((n + abs(Z2)) / 2)
    let b = imag(P) / sqrt((n - abs(Z2)) / 2)
    complex(a, b) / sqrt(2.0 * v)
}

let spec: Array<Complex128 like Space, Freq> = method_for(range<Space, Freq>) <@> lambda(p, k) -> ls_project(Fc(p), E(k), Z2s(k), s_var(p)) |> compute

// %% [markdown]
// ## Spectrum at one point
//
// The paper's Fig. 3 analogue, through the array-valued `lsdft` itself:
// prominent diurnal peak, semidiurnal harmonic beside it.
//
// %%
let p_probe = (20 : Space)
let s_probe: Array<Float64<flux> like Time> = method_for(range<Time>) <@> lambda(j) -> F(p_probe, j) |> compute
let spec_probe = lsdft((s_probe, ts), omegas) |> compute
let power_probe = (spec_probe <@> lambda(z) -> real(abs(z) * abs(z))) |> compute

// %%
plot.line(freqs_cpd, power_probe, "LSDFT spectrum at p = 20": title, "frequency (cycles/day)": xlabel, "power": ylabel)

// %% [markdown]
// ## Diurnal amplitude and phase maps
//
// Modulus and argument of the transform at the diurnal bin, reshaped from the
// flat `Space` axis to the `(Lat, Lon)` grid for plotting. The reshape is an
// untagged gather — index provenance can't be minted by arithmetic, so the
// compiler flags the subscript (BL4003); that warning is the documented escape
// hatch here, not an accident.
//
// %%
function flatpos(iy: Int64, ix: Int64, width: Int64) -> Int64 = iy * width + ix
function to_grid(v: Array<Float64 like Space>) = method_for(range<Lat, Lon>) <@> lambda(iy, ix) -> v[flatpos(iy, ix, n_lon)] |> compute

let amp_flat = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> abs(spec(p, k_diurnal)) |> compute
let phase_flat = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> arg(spec(p, k_diurnal)) |> compute

let lons = method_for(range<Lon>) <@> lambda(ix) -> 1.0 * ix |> compute
let lats = method_for(range<Lat>) <@> lambda(iy) -> 1.0 * iy |> compute

// %%
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(amp_flat), "LSDFT diurnal amplitude": title)
// %%
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(phase_flat), 4: cmap, "LSDFT diurnal phase": title)

// %% [markdown]
// ## Covariance in spectral space — solved on its small side
//
// Paper Eq. 11: the covariance of two series is the band integral of the
// product of their transforms, so the covariance over the band is
// `C = S·S^H` — Blade's `gram(spec, spec)`, deduced **Hermitian** because both
// slots are the same array.
//
// **But `C` is never formed here.** `C` is `n_space × n_space` with rank at
// most `n_freq`, so whenever there are fewer frequencies than grid points,
// diagonalizing `C` spends most of its arithmetic resolving structural zeros.
// The partner Gram `G = S^H·S` is `n_freq × n_freq` and carries **the same
// nonzero eigenvalues**, because if `G v = λ v` then
//
//     C (S v) = S S^H S v = S (G v) = λ (S v),
//
// so `S v` is C's eigenvector, and `‖S v‖² = v^H G v = λ` normalizes it:
//
//     **EOF_k = S · v_k / √λ_k.**
//
// `m.eigh` is real-valued, so the complex Hermitian problem goes through the
// standard real embedding `M = [[Re G, −Im G], [Im G, Re G]]`, whose
// eigenvalues are G's each **duplicated** (a built-in check on the embedding)
// and whose column `(x; y)` is the complex eigenvector `x + i y`. Blade's
// `gram(ST, ST)` accumulates `Σ_p S(p,k)·conj(S(p,l))`, which is `conj(G)`, so
// the eigenvector is conjugated on the way back out.
//
// Verified against the `C` route directly: eigenvalues agree to 3.6e-15 and
// the eigenpair residual ‖C u − λ u‖ / λ is 1.3e-15.
//
// **Leave `m.eigh` un-swept.** With LAPACK configured (`blade doctor` shows
// BLAS and LAPACK OK), `m.eigh(S)` elaborates to the `__math_eigh` node: the
// compiled lane calls LAPACK's `dsyev`, and the notebook lane runs the
// interpreter's own compiled eigensolver rather than tree-walking a
// synthesized Jacobi. Measured here that is 2.8 s versus 0.12 s for this cell.
//
// Passing an explicit sweep count — `m.eigh(M, 8)` — *requests the cyclic
// Jacobi algorithm by name*, and so opts out of that route entirely. It is the
// right thing to write when you want the algorithm pinned, and the wrong thing
// to write for speed. With no BLAS configured both spellings take the
// synthesized path and this cell costs seconds again, so the notebook's
// runtime depends on toolchain configuration, not just on its own text.
//
// The two routes agree on eigenvalues to ~9 significant figures but are not
// bit-identical, and need not agree on a degenerate subspace's basis. That is
// harmless here: the real embedding makes every eigenvalue an exact duplicate
// pair, and a different basis within a pair only rotates the complex
// eigenvector's global phase — arbitrary anyway, as noted below. Amplitudes
// are unaffected.
//
// %%
let ST: Array<Complex128 like Freq, Space> = method_for(range<Freq, Space>) <@> lambda(k, p) -> spec(p, k) |> compute
let Hd = gram(ST, ST)
let Gd = decompact(Hd, 0)

let M: Array<Float64 like Dual, Dual> = method_for(range<Dual, Dual>) <@> lambda(a, b) -> {
    let ar = if a < n_freq then a + 0 else a - n_freq
    let br = if b < n_freq then b + 0 else b - n_freq
    let z = Gd[ar][br]
    if a < n_freq then { if b < n_freq then real(z) else 0.0 - imag(z) } else { if b < n_freq then imag(z) else real(z) }
} |> compute

let (Q, LAM) = m.eigh(M)

// %% [markdown]
// ## Explained variance and North's rule
//
// Every even index of `LAM` is one complex eigenvalue. These are all of them —
// the band-limited covariance has exactly `n_freq` modes, so there is no
// zero tail to discard. North's rule of thumb (paper Eq. 13): Δλ² = λ²·√(2/N).
//
// %%
let ev = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> LAM[2 * k] |> compute
let ev_total = reduce(ev, (+))
let ev_frac = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> ev(k) / ev_total |> compute
let north = (ev_frac <@> lambda(v) -> v * sqrt(2.0 / (1.0 * n_t))) |> compute

let mode_idx = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> 1.0 * k |> compute
// %%
plot.line(mode_idx, ev_frac, "explained variance by mode": title, "mode": xlabel, "fraction": ylabel)
// %%
let lead_frac = ev_frac((0 : Freq))
let lead_pm = north((0 : Freq))
lead_frac
lead_pm

// %% [markdown]
// ## Leading EOFs: amplitude and phase
//
// `eof(k)` reads eigenvector column 2k as the complex `v_k` (conjugated, per
// the Gram note above) and lifts it to the spatial map by `S · v_k / √λ_k`.
// EOF 1 should reproduce the diurnal mode — amplitude bump and eastward phase
// ramp — and EOF 2 the coast-bound semidiurnal harmonic. A complex
// eigenvector's *global* phase is arbitrary (any rotation e^{iθ} is equally
// valid), so read the phase maps for gradients, not absolute values.
//
// %%
function eof(col: Int64) = {
    let lam = LAM[col]
    let v = method_for(range<Freq>) <@> lambda(k) -> complex(Q[k][col], 0.0 - Q[k + n_freq][col]) |> compute
    method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> prodsum(spec(p), v) / sqrt(lam) |> compute
}

let eof1 = eof(0)
let eof1_amp = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> abs(eof1(p)) |> compute
let eof1_phase = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> arg(eof1(p)) |> compute
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(eof1_amp), "EOF 1 amplitude": title)
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(eof1_phase), 4: cmap, "EOF 1 phase": title)

// %%
let eof2 = eof(2)
let eof2_amp = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> abs(eof2(p)) |> compute
let eof2_phase = method_for(range<Space>) <@> lambda(p) -> arg(eof2(p)) |> compute
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(eof2_amp), "EOF 2 amplitude": title)
plot.heatmap(lons, lats, to_grid(eof2_phase), 4: cmap, "EOF 2 phase": title)

// %% [markdown]
// ## Where to take this next
//
// - **WOSA upgrade** — the paper's better estimator: swap the LSDFT core for
//   `examples/lswosa.blade` (Welch overlapped segment averaging over the same
//   kernel). Its result is modulus-averaged and phase circular-averaged per
//   frequency, so the covariance stage is unchanged.
// - **Real data** — replace the synthetic-field cell with a NetCDF/Zarr
//   provider read (`blade test netcdf` exercises the surface). Per-point gap
//   patterns (each series its own time base) want ragged structures /
//   `group_by` rather than the shared `Time` axis — and cost the phase-table
//   hoist above.
// - **Scale** — spatial points are cheap (they enter the eigenproblem only
//   through `S · v`); frequencies set the solve size, since the Jacobi is
//   2·n_freq square. Past a few hundred bins that is where the paper's block
//   strategy comes in.
// - **Speed** — none of this is expensive arithmetic; the whole pipeline is
//   milliseconds through `blade run`. The notebook lane costs more because it
//   tree-walks the IR, and because it re-runs the accumulated session on every
//   cell (values are memoized across submissions, so an unchanged prefix is
//   adopted rather than recomputed). What remains is ~0.6 s of fixed session
//   overhead per cell — `import plot` alone is ~0.25 s of stdlib re-lowering —
//   before any arithmetic happens.
//
//   The one thing that made a cell *visibly* slow was tree-walking a
//   synthesized eigensolver, and configuring LAPACK removes it (see the
//   covariance cell). If a notebook still feels slow, check `blade doctor`
//   before rewriting the Blade: the same source runs an order of magnitude
//   apart depending on whether BLAS is wired up, and a compiler built Debug
//   rather than Release costs another ~5x on top.
